# proxmox-tools

`proxmox-tool` is a command-line tool for querying Proxmox VE clusters through the REST API, without the web UI.

Full usage, columns and limitations: [user-guide.md](user-guide.md).

## Commands

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `context add/list/use/current` | Manage configured clusters and the active context |
| `get nodes` | Nodes with live CPU/RAM usage, uptime and running VM count |
| `get vms` | VM table with filters (`--pool`, `--without-pool`, `--node`, `--status`, `--tag`), optional `--serial` column and summary |
| `get vm --ip/--serial/--name` | Find VMs by IP (Guest Agent), SMBIOS serial or name |
| `get firewall` | VM-level and per-interface firewall switches |
| `describe vm <vmid>` | General, resources, network, disks, snapshots, last backup, guest OS |

## Architecture

- Proxmox VE REST API (`https://<host>:8006/api2/json`), API token authentication.
- Multiple clusters with one active context, similar to `kubectl config use-context`.
- Live data only, no cache. Per-VM requests run in parallel (up to 20).
- Configuration and secrets in `~/.config/proxmox-tools/config.yaml` (mode `600`).
- Python, Typer, requests, rich, questionary, PyYAML.

## Requirements

- Python 3.10+ and `pipx` on the workstation.
- HTTPS access to the cluster API (port 8006).
- API token with the `PVEAuditor` role and its own ACL entry (see [user-guide.md](user-guide.md#requirements)).
- `qemu-guest-agent` in VMs for IP lookup and live data in `describe vm`.

## Installation

```bash
pipx install .
```

Upgrade: `pipx install --force .`

